    "introduction": "Nagara temple architecture developed across Northern and Central India from the 5th century CE onwards. It is characterized by a square sanctum (Garbhagriha), a cruciform ground plan (Triratha to Saptaratha), and a towering curvilinear beehive-shaped spire (Shikhara / Rekha-Deula) capped by a fluted ribbed stone disc (Amalaka) and a pot finial (Kalasha). Unlike southern temples, Nagara temples generally lack elaborate boundary walls and monumental gopurams.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Defining Elements of the Nagara Style</h3>\n<p>Nagara temples are elevated on a high stone plinth (Jagati) approached by steps:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Garbhagriha (Womb Chamber):</strong> The dark, square inner sanctum housing the principal icon.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mandapa:</strong> Pillared entrance hall for congregation and rituals.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shikhara:</strong> Curvilinear tower rising gracefully above the sanctum, adorned with miniature repetitive urushringas (turrets).</li>\n  <li><strong>Amalaka & Kalasha:</strong> The crowning ribbed cushion stone and water-pot finial.</li>\n  <li><strong>Panchayatana Layout:</strong> Main shrine surrounded symmetrically by four subsidiary subordinate shrines (seen early at <strong>Dashavatara Temple, Deogarh, UP</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Prominent Regional Sub-Schools</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Central Indian (Khajuraho, Chandella Dynasty):</strong> Temples constructed on elevated platforms with integrated circumambulatory paths (Sandhara) and exquisitely carved erotic and celestial sculptures (e.g., Kandariya Mahadeva Temple).</li>\n  <li><strong>Odisha School (Kalinga Style):</strong> Composed of two mandatory architectural parts: the sanctum tower (<em>Rekha Deula</em>), the dance hall (<em>Nata Mandapa</em>), the offering hall (<em>Bhoga Mandapa</em>), and the tiered pyramidal assembly hall (<em>Jagamohana</em> / Pidha Deula). Exemplified by Lingaraja Temple (Bhubaneswar), Jagannath Temple (Puri), and the Sun Temple (Konark).</li>\n  <li><strong>Solanki / Maru-Gurjara School (Gujarat & Rajasthan):</strong> Famous for step-wells, intricately carved white marble ceilings, and detached Torana archways. Exemplified by the Sun Temple at Modhera (with its Surya Kund) and Dilwara Jain Temples at Mount Abu.</li>\n</ul>",
