    "introduction": "Dravida temple architecture reached sublime artistic heights in southern India from the 7th to 17th centuries. Distinguished by a stepped pyramidal tower (Vimana) rising in diminishing storeys (talas) capped by an octagonal or domical dome (Shikhara/Stupika), enclosed by high boundary walls (Prakara), monumental entrance gateway towers (Gopurams), pillared corridors (Mandapas), and sacred water tanks (Kalyani).",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Evolutionary Stages of Dravidian Architecture</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Pallava Phase (7th\u20138th Century):</strong> Initiated rock-cut cave temples (Mandapas) by Mahendravarman I, monolithic rock-cut shrines (Pancha Rathas at Mahabalipuram by Narasimhavarman I Mamalla), and early structural stone temples (Shore Temple, Kanchi Kailasanatha by Rajasimha).</li>\n  <li><strong>Imperial Chola Pinnacle (10th\u201311th Century):</strong> The Vimana dominated the architectural silhouette over the sanctum. Exemplified by the Brihadisvara Temple at Thanjavur (consecrated 1010 CE by Rajaraja I; 216-foot granite Vimana) and Gangaikondacholapuram (Rajendra I).</li>\n  <li><strong>Pandya & Vijayanagara Expansion (13th\u201316th Century):</strong> Shifting aesthetic prominence from the central Vimana to towering multi-tiered gateway towers (Raya Gopurams) and massive thousand-pillared halls (Kalyana Mandapas) adorned with rearing yalis (mythical beasts), as seen at Hampi (Virupaksha Temple, Vittala Temple stone chariot).</li>\n  <li><strong>Madurai Nayaka Grandeur (17th Century):</strong> Characterized by concentric prakara corridors and towering outer Gopurams (Meenakshi Sundareswarar Temple, Madurai).</li>\n</ul>",
