    "subtitle": "Greco-Buddhist Schist, Spotted Red Sandstone, Chunar Cream Sandstone & Palnadu White Marble",
    "learningObjective": "Compare the four classical sculptural schools of ancient India: their material media, stylistic traits, religious themes, and patronage.",
    "introduction": "The 1st to 6th centuries CE witnessed the emergence of four major sculptural schools across the subcontinent. Gandhara in the northwest blended Hellenistic realism with Buddhist iconology. Mathura in Uttar Pradesh pioneered indigenous anthropomorphic depictions of Buddha, Tirthankaras, and Brahmanical deities using red mottled sandstone. Sarnath during the Gupta era perfected classical spiritual serenity, while Amaravati in the Krishna valley celebrated dynamic narrative reliefs on white limestone.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Gandhara School of Art (Greco-Buddhist)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Geography & Patronage:</strong> Northwestern frontier (Peshwar, Taxila, Swat); patronized by Kushana kings (Kanishka).</li>\n  <li><strong>Medium:</strong> Bluish-grey schist stone and later stucco / terracotta (Hadda, Taxila).</li>\n  <li><strong>Aesthetic Style:</strong> Realistic Greco-Roman features: wavy hair tied in Apollo-like topknot (Ushnisha), heavy realistic drapery folds, sharp facial contours, mustache, and sandals.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Mathura School of Art (Indigenous Center in UP)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Geography & Patronage:</strong> Centered at Mathura on the Yamuna in Uttar Pradesh; patronized by Kushanas and Guptas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Medium:</strong> Distinctive local <strong>red mottled sandstone</strong> with white spots from Sikri.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ecumenical Character:</strong> Created images across all three faiths: Buddhism (corpulent Buddha with shaved head, right hand in Abhaya Mudra), Jainism (Ayagapatas and seated Tirthankaras), and Hinduism (earliest images of Shiva Lingas, Vishnu, Kartikeya, Mahishasuramardini).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Sarnath Gupta Classical School (UP)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Medium:</strong> Chunar cream-colored buff sandstone with delicate metallic polish.</li>\n  <li><strong>Aesthetic Zenith:</strong> Ethereal spiritual introspection, transparent clings-to-body drapery devoid of folds, smooth rounded limbs, and elaborately decorated floral halos (Prabhavali). Masterpiece: The <strong>Dharmachakra Pravartana Buddha</strong> of Sarnath.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4. Amaravati School of Art</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Geography & Patronage:</strong> Lower Krishna-Godavari valley (Andhra); patronized by Satavahanas and Ikshvakus.</li>\n  <li><strong>Medium:</strong> White crystalline limestone (marble-like).</li>\n  <li><strong>Hallmark:</strong> Dynamic narrative friezes depicting Jataka tales, crowded animated compositions, and slender flexed human postures.</li>\n</ul>",
